IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Child Support (Registration and Collection) Act 1988 (Commonwealth) -- how child support is collected from the paying parent:
-- whether the Registrar collects or the parents deal privately (ss 17, 24, 24A, 25, 30, 37B, 38, 38A, 38B, 39); employer
-- withholding from my pay (ss 43, 44, 45, 46, 47(1)-(2), 49, 50, 57, 65AA); when my debts fall due and the late payment penalty
-- (ss 66, 67, 68); credits for payments I make other than to the Registrar (ss 71, 71C); collection from my tax refund, from
-- people who hold my money and from my Centrelink payment (ss 72, 72A, 72AA); departure prohibition orders (ss 72D, 72E, 72F,
-- 72I, 72L); recovery in court (ss 113, 113A); and where this Act stands when I am in a debt agreement or my employer is bankrupt.
--
-- SEAT: the paying parent -- "How can child support be collected from me?"
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 77, compilation date 1 July 2026 (includes amendments up to Act No. 17, 2025), Federal Register of
-- Legislation register id C2026C00287, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A03596.txt. The Child Support
-- (Registration and Collection) Regulations 2018 are NOT deposited: the protected earnings rate (s 4(1)), the prescribed periodic
-- deduction from a social security payment (s 72AA), the kinds of payment credited under s 71C and the satisfactory payment record
-- (s 38B) are INPUTS. The general interest charge rate (Taxation Administration Act 1953 s 8AAD(1)) that s 67 borrows is an
-- INPUT. Penalties are given in penalty units; their dollar value is set by the Crimes Act 1914 s 4AA, not read.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: the amount of child support (Child Support (Assessment) Act 1989); registration of overseas liabilities (s 18A),
-- transferred liabilities (ss 20-22) and s 17A/s 18 liabilities; the content of the Register (ss 26-29) beyond the payment period;
-- the s 37B low-income period's start and end days; s 39A arrears after re-enforcement; s 39B; employers' records, penalties and
-- offences (ss 47(3)-(4), 48, 51-56, 58-63) except as they reach the payer; s 69 (judgment interest), s 69A (lump sums), s 69B
-- (carer debts), s 70, ss 71AA, 71AB, 71A, 71B, 71D (beyond an input), 71E; deductions from family tax benefit, veterans' payments
-- and parental leave pay (ss 72AB-72AD); ss 72B, 72C; departure procedure (ss 72G, 72H, 72J, 72K, 72M-72Y); the Account (Part VI);
-- suspension (Part VIA); objections, ART review and appeals (Parts VII-VIII); secrecy, regulations and administration.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the seat of the individual to whom the law is applied, with the
-- design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use
-- as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Child Support (Registration and Collection) Act 1988 -- collection, from the paying parent's seat`

§§ `helpers`

GIVEN `first amount` IS A NUMBER, `second amount` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` MEANS
    IF `first amount` LESS THAN `second amount` THEN `first amount` ELSE `second amount`

GIVEN `an amount` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`not below nil` MEANS
    IF `an amount` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `an amount`

§§ `ss 17, 24, 24A, 25 -- is my liability registered`

-- s 17(1): a "registrable maintenance liability" is a parent's liability "to pay a periodic amount ... for the maintenance of the
-- child" that "(i) arises under a court order or court registered maintenance agreement; (ii) ... is a collection agency
-- maintenance liability". s 17(2): also a liability "if it arises under a child support assessment".
-- (Gloss: "registrable" means the Registrar may take it over; "registered" means the Registrar has.)
DECLARE `Liability basis` IS ONE OF
    `a child support assessment`
    `a court order or court registered maintenance agreement`
    `a collection agency maintenance liability`

-- s 24A(1): on making a child support assessment the Registrar "must immediately register the liability". s 24A(2): not if "(b)
-- the payee elected in the relevant application ... not to have the liability enforced under this Act; or (c) the application
-- giving rise to the child support assessment was made by the parent by whom child support is payable".
-- s 23(2), s 24(1): for a court order or agreement the payee must notify the Registrar within 14 days, and the Registrar "shall,
-- within 28 days after receipt of the notice, register the liability"; s 23(3): unless the payee "elects ... not to have the
-- registrable maintenance liability enforced under this Act".
-- s 25(1), (2): the payee of a liability "that is not registered under this Act may apply"; the Registrar "must, within 28 days
-- after receiving the application, register the liability".
-- s 24(2) (registration although the payee never notified) is a power, not modelled; transferred collection agency liabilities
-- (ss 20-22) are not modelled, so such a liability is registered here only on a s 25 application.
@export Is my child support liability registered for collection by the Child Support Registrar?
GIVEN `the basis` IS A `Liability basis` @desc What the liability arises under: an assessment, a court order or court registered agreement, or a State collection agency liability
      `the payee elected not to have it enforced under this Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other parent (the payee) chose, in the assessment application or within 14 days of the order, private collection
      `I made the application for assessment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, the paying parent, applied for the child support assessment
      `the payee notified the Registrar of the order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a court order or agreement: whether the payee gave the Registrar the s 23(2) notice
      `the payee applied to register it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the payee has applied to the Registrar under s 25 to register the liability (the Registrar then has 28 days)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my liability registered` IF
        `the payee applied to register it`
    OR  CONSIDER `the basis`
        WHEN `a child support assessment` THEN
                (NOT `the payee elected not to have it enforced under this Act`) AND (NOT `I made the application for assessment`)
        WHEN `a court order or court registered maintenance agreement` THEN
                `the payee notified the Registrar of the order` AND (NOT `the payee elected not to have it enforced under this Act`)
        OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `ss 30, 38, 38A, 38B, 39 -- who collects`

-- s 30(1): once registered, the amounts payable "are debts due to the Commonwealth by the payer". s 38: the Registrar "must vary
-- the particulars ... so that the liability is no longer enforced under this Act if: (a) the payee ... makes an election under
-- section 38A; or (b) the payee and payer ... jointly make an election under section 38A; or (c) the Registrar makes a decision
-- under section 38B". s 39(1): the payee "may apply to the Registrar for the liability to again become enforceable".
-- I cannot end Registrar collection alone: s 38A gives the election to the payee, or the payee and payer jointly.
DECLARE `Collection route` IS ONE OF
    `privately -- my liability is not registered, so this Act does not collect it`
    `privately -- my liability is registered but no longer enforced under this Act`
    `by the Registrar -- the amounts are debts I owe the Commonwealth`

@export Who collects my child support -- the Child Support Registrar, or is it private collection?
GIVEN `my liability is registered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the liability is registered under this Act
      `a s 38A election or s 38B decision has taken effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the payee (alone or with you) elected, or the Registrar decided on your payment record, that the Registrar stop enforcing it, and the terminating day has come
      `a s 39 application to collect again has taken effect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, after that, the payee's application for the Registrar to collect again was granted and its day has come
GIVETH A `Collection route`
DECIDE `How is my child support collected` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `my liability is registered`
            THEN `privately -- my liability is not registered, so this Act does not collect it`
        IF `a s 38A election or s 38B decision has taken effect` AND (NOT `a s 39 application to collect again has taken effect`)
            THEN `privately -- my liability is registered but no longer enforced under this Act`
        OTHERWISE `by the Registrar -- the amounts are debts I owe the Commonwealth`

-- s 39(3): no application "during a period that is a low-income non-enforcement period under section 37B". s 39(5): "The
-- Registrar must grant the application unless the Registrar is satisfied that: (a) the payer ... has been complying with his or
-- her child support obligations ...; or (b) the payer ... has satisfactorily explained and rectified a failure to comply ...; or
-- (c) there are special circumstances ...". s 39(4): decided within 28 days; s 39(6)(a): enforceable again from a day "not later
-- than 60 days after the day on which the Registrar received the application".
DECLARE `Return to Registrar collection` IS ONE OF
    `the Registrar must grant it`
    `the Registrar may refuse it`
    `the payee cannot apply during my low-income non-enforcement period`

@export If the other parent asks the Registrar to collect from me again, must it be granted?
GIVEN `it is my low-income non-enforcement period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a s 37B low-income non-enforcement period is running for the liability
      `the Registrar is satisfied I have been complying` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied you have been complying with your child support obligations to the payee
      `the Registrar is satisfied I explained and rectified a failure` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied you have satisfactorily explained and put right a failure to comply
      `the Registrar is satisfied there are special circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied special circumstances make refusal appropriate
GIVETH A `Return to Registrar collection`
DECIDE `Must the payee's application to collect again be granted` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `it is my low-income non-enforcement period`
            THEN `the payee cannot apply during my low-income non-enforcement period`
        IF     `the Registrar is satisfied I have been complying`
            OR `the Registrar is satisfied I explained and rectified a failure`
            OR `the Registrar is satisfied there are special circumstances`
            THEN `the Registrar may refuse it`
        OTHERWISE `the Registrar must grant it`

@export If the payee's application is granted, what is the latest day the Registrar can start collecting from me again?
GIVEN `the day the Registrar received the application` IS A DATE @desc The day the Registrar received the payee's s 39 application
GIVETH A DATE
`the latest day Registrar collection starts again` MEANS `the day the Registrar received the application` PLUS 60

-- s 38A(3)(b): if the payee, or the payee and payer jointly, "have elected to have amounts payable ... that are unpaid on the
-- terminating day also no longer enforced under this Act", the Registrar varies the Register "so as to ensure that, in spite of
-- section 30, those amounts cease to be debts due by the payer to the Commonwealth". Nothing else in the Act cancels arrears when
-- enforcement ends; s 45(4)(c) keeps a wage notice in force for "any liability of the payer ... to pay a child support debt that
-- relates to the registered maintenance liability" after it "ceases to be enforceable". Whether arrears dropped under s 38A(3)(b)
-- become payable to the payee is not said, except for overseas liabilities (s 38C).
DECLARE `How enforcement ended` IS ONE OF
    `a s 38A election that includes the unpaid amounts`
    `a s 38A election that does not include the unpaid amounts`
    `a Registrar decision under s 38B`

DECLARE `Arrears outcome` IS ONE OF
    `my arrears stay debts to the Commonwealth and the Registrar can still collect them`
    `my arrears cease to be debts to the Commonwealth`

@export When the Registrar stops collecting, what happens to the child support I have not yet paid?
GIVEN `how it ended` IS A `How enforcement ended` @desc How Registrar enforcement ended: the payee's (or a joint) s 38A election, with or without the unpaid amounts, or a Registrar decision under s 38B
GIVETH A `Arrears outcome`
DECIDE `What happens to my arrears when Registrar collection ends` IS
    CONSIDER `how it ended`
        WHEN `a s 38A election that includes the unpaid amounts` THEN `my arrears cease to be debts to the Commonwealth`
        OTHERWISE `my arrears stay debts to the Commonwealth and the Registrar can still collect them`

-- s 37B(2): "If the payer of an enforceable maintenance liability covered by subsection 17(1) ... (a) is in receipt of a social
-- security pension or a social security benefit; or (b) has made a claim for [one] ... the payer may apply to the Registrar ...
-- to have the liability no longer enforced under this Act." s 37B(3): not if the liability is under a court order or agreement
-- that "provides ... for the reduction of amounts payable ... during periods when: (i) the payer is unemployed; or (ii) the
-- payer's income is substantially reduced". Subsection 17(1) is the court order route: an assessment (s 17(2)) is not covered.
-- (Whether the period then starts turns on the regulations' income test, s 37B(4): not encoded.)
@export May I apply to stop Registrar enforcement because I am on, or have claimed, a Centrelink pension or benefit?
GIVEN `the basis` IS A `Liability basis` @desc What the liability arises under
      `I receive or have claimed a social security pension or benefit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are paid, or have claimed, a social security pension or benefit (Social Security Act 1991 meanings)
      `the order provides for reduction if I am unemployed or my income falls` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a court order or agreement: whether it already reduces the amount while you are unemployed or your income is substantially reduced
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I apply for low-income non-enforcement` IF
        `I receive or have claimed a social security pension or benefit`
    AND CONSIDER `the basis`
        WHEN `a child support assessment` THEN FALSE
        WHEN `a court order or court registered maintenance agreement` THEN NOT `the order provides for reduction if I am unemployed or my income falls`
        OTHERWISE TRUE

§ `Part IV -- collection from my pay`

§§ `ss 43, 65AA -- employer withholding`

-- s 43(1): applies if "an amount payable under or in relation to ... (i) an enforceable maintenance liability; (ii) a liability to
-- pay a child support debt; (iii) ... a child support related debt; (iv) a carer liability ... is due by the payer to the
-- Commonwealth; and (b) the payer is an employee". s 43(2): "The Registrar must, as far as practicable, collect amounts due ... by
-- deduction from the salary or wages of the payer under this Part." s 43(3): not if the Register says "employer withholding does
-- not apply". s 4(1): "employee ... means a person who receives, or is entitled to receive, work and income support related
-- withholding payments" (a Taxation Administration Act 1953 term: asked as a fact). s 65AA: "This Part applies to a person ...
-- engaged under a contract for services as if ... a reference to an employee includes a reference to the independent contractor".
@export Can child support be taken from my pay -- am I someone Part IV reaches?
GIVEN `I receive pay subject to PAYG withholding` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you receive, or are entitled to, payments from which tax must be withheld (wages, director's fees, and so on)
      `I am engaged under a contract for services` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you work as an independent contractor for someone who engages you
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I an employee for Part IV` IF
    `I receive pay subject to PAYG withholding` OR `I am engaged under a contract for services`

-- s 44(1): on the payer's election, if "the Registrar is satisfied that the payer is likely to make timely payments", the Register
-- is varied to say employer withholding does not apply. s 44(6): likewise in "special circumstances" where it "would not be an
-- efficient method". s 44(5), (7): if "the payer does not make timely payments ... the Registrar must vary those particulars so
-- that they contain a statement that employer withholding applies", unless (s 44(5A), (7A)) the Registrar is satisfied withholding
-- "would not be an efficient method" or "the payer is likely to recommence timely payments ... in the near future". s 44(8): a
-- special-circumstances statement goes when the Registrar "becomes satisfied" withholding would be efficient. s 44(10): if the
-- period at which amounts are payable "exceeds one calendar month or one month, the Registrar shall include ... a statement that
-- employer withholding does not apply".
@export Does employer withholding apply to my child support?
GIVEN `the liability's period is longer than a month` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order or assessment makes amounts payable at a period longer than a month
      `my election to pay the Registrar directly was accepted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you elected under s 44(1) or (2) that employer withholding not apply and the Registrar accepted it
      `the Registrar made a special-circumstances statement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar decided under s 44(6) that withholding would not be efficient for you
      `I have since failed to make timely payments` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, since that statement, you have not paid the Registrar on time
      `the Registrar is satisfied withholding is inefficient or I will soon pay on time again` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied withholding would not be efficient or that you are likely to resume timely payments soon
      `the Registrar has become satisfied withholding would be efficient` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a special-circumstances statement: whether the Registrar is now satisfied withholding would be efficient
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does employer withholding apply` IF
        (NOT `the liability's period is longer than a month`)
    AND (    ((NOT `my election to pay the Registrar directly was accepted`) AND (NOT `the Registrar made a special-circumstances statement`))
         OR  (`I have since failed to make timely payments` AND (NOT `the Registrar is satisfied withholding is inefficient or I will soon pay on time again`))
         OR  (`the Registrar made a special-circumstances statement` AND `the Registrar has become satisfied withholding would be efficient`))

@export Must the Registrar collect my child support by deduction from my pay?
GIVEN `an amount is due from me to the Commonwealth` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an amount under an enforceable liability, a child support debt, a late payment penalty or a carer liability is due from you
      `I am an employee for Part IV` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are an employee or contractor Part IV reaches
      `employer withholding applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Register does not say employer withholding does not apply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the Registrar collect from my pay` IF
    `an amount is due from me to the Commonwealth` AND `I am an employee for Part IV` AND `employer withholding applies`

-- s 44(7B): no election "if, within the previous 6 months, employer withholding started to apply ... because of a variation made
-- under subsection (5) or (7)". s 44(7C): none "within 2 months after" a rejected election. s 44(1): it takes effect only if the
-- Registrar "is satisfied that the payer is likely to make timely payments".
DECLARE `Opt-out answer` IS ONE OF
    `I may elect -- it takes effect if the Registrar is satisfied I am likely to pay on time`
    `not yet -- withholding restarted for missed payments less than 6 months ago`
    `not yet -- my last election was rejected less than 2 months ago`

@export May I elect to stop deductions from my pay and pay the Registrar myself?
GIVEN `withholding restarted under s 44(5) or (7)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether withholding started again because you missed timely payments
      `months since it restarted` IS A NUMBER @desc Whole months since withholding restarted (ignored if it did not)
      `my last election was rejected` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar rejected an earlier election
      `months since the rejection` IS A NUMBER @desc Whole months since that rejection (ignored if none)
GIVETH A `Opt-out answer`
DECIDE `May I elect that employer withholding not apply` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `withholding restarted under s 44(5) or (7)` AND `months since it restarted` LESS THAN 6
            THEN `not yet -- withholding restarted for missed payments less than 6 months ago`
        IF `my last election was rejected` AND `months since the rejection` LESS THAN 2
            THEN `not yet -- my last election was rejected less than 2 months ago`
        OTHERWISE `I may elect -- it takes effect if the Registrar is satisfied I am likely to pay on time`

§§ `s 46 -- how much my employer deducts`

-- s 46(2): "the amount of the deduction is the amount ascertained by applying the weekly deduction rate specified in the notice to
-- the period in relation to which the salary or wages are paid". s 46(4): "Where the amount of the salary or wages exceeds the
-- amount of the deduction ... by less than the amount (the protected earnings amount) ascertained by applying the protected
-- earnings rate to the period ..., the amount of the deduction is the amount by which the amount of the salary or wages exceeds the
-- protected earnings amount." s 46(8): salary or wages are taken "after amounts (if any) that are required to be withheld" as PAYG.
-- s 4(1): "protected earnings rate means a weekly rate prescribed" -- the Regulations are not deposited, so it is an INPUT.
-- The rate is applied to the period as days divided by 7 (s 46(6)(c) divides longer periods into weeks by days). Where pay is below
-- the protected earnings amount, s 46(4) read literally does not apply (pay does not "exceed" the deduction) and s 46(2) would take
-- the full rate; this encoding takes nothing, reading (4) and (4B) together. (Inference from the words.)
@export How much child support can my employer deduct from this pay?
GIVEN `the weekly deduction rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The weekly rate in the Registrar's notice to your employer
      `days in the pay period` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days this pay covers (7 weekly, 14 fortnightly)
      `my pay after tax withheld` IS A NUMBER @desc Your salary or wages for the period after PAYG tax is withheld
      `the protected earnings rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The weekly protected earnings rate prescribed by the Regulations (not deposited: give the current figure)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How much can my employer deduct from this pay` IS
    IF `my pay after tax withheld` MINUS `the full deduction` LESS THAN `the protected earnings amount`
    THEN `not below nil` (`my pay after tax withheld` MINUS `the protected earnings amount`)
    ELSE `the full deduction`
    WHERE
        `the full deduction` MEANS `the weekly deduction rate` TIMES `days in the pay period` DIVIDED BY 7
        `the protected earnings amount` MEANS `the protected earnings rate` TIMES `days in the pay period` DIVIDED BY 7

§§ `ss 47, 49, 50 -- after the deduction`

-- s 47(1): an employer who "has during any calendar month made deductions ... shall, not later than the seventh day of the
-- following calendar month: (a) pay to the Registrar the amounts so deducted". s 47(2): on each payment the employer "shall notify
-- the employee, in writing: (a) of the amount of the deduction ...; or (b) that no deduction has been made".
@export By when must my employer pay what it deducted from me to the Registrar?
GIVEN `the day of the deduction` IS A DATE @desc The pay day on which the deduction was made
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must my employer remit my deduction` MEANS
    IF DATE_MONTH `the day of the deduction` EQUALS 12
    THEN Date 7 1 (DATE_YEAR `the day of the deduction` PLUS 1)
    ELSE Date 7 (DATE_MONTH `the day of the deduction` PLUS 1) (DATE_YEAR `the day of the deduction`)

-- s 49: "Where an employer deducts an amount ... (a) the payer is, to the extent of the amount deducted, discharged from the
-- payer's liability to make payments to the Registrar". The discharge happens at deduction, whether or not the employer remits.
-- s 50(1)-(2): if the employer's property passes to "a trustee", the trustee "is liable to pay the amount to the Registrar", with
-- priority. So my employer's late remittance or bankruptcy leaves me discharged.
@export After my employer deducts child support from my pay, how much do I still owe?
GIVEN `what I owed` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount due from you before the deduction
      `what my employer deducted` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount deducted from your pay (whether or not your employer has paid it over)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much do I still owe after the deduction` MEANS `not below nil` (`what I owed` MINUS `what my employer deducted`)

§§ `s 57 -- my employer must not punish me for it`

-- s 57(1): an employer who "(a) refuses to employ, or to pay salary or wages to, another person; (b) dismisses, or threatens to
-- dismiss ...; (c) terminates ... the payment of salary or wages ...; (d) prejudices ... another person in the other person's
-- employment ...; or (e) intimidates or coerces, imposes any pecuniary or other penalty on, or takes any other disciplinary action"
-- because the person "(f) is the payer of a registrable maintenance liability or any other deductible liability; or (g) is an
-- employee in relation to whom a notice has been given ... under subsection 45(1)" commits an offence, "a fine not exceeding 20
-- penalty units". s 57(2): no need to prove the reason, but a defence if the employer proves "the action was not motivated (either
-- in whole or in part)" by it. s 57(3): the court may order compensation to me.
@export If my employer sacks or penalises me, does it commit an offence because of my child support?
GIVEN `my employer took an action listed in s 57(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer refused to employ or pay you, dismissed or threatened you, prejudiced you, or disciplined you
      `the employer proves child support played no part in it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employer proves, on the balance of probabilities, the action was not motivated even in part by your child support liability or the deduction notice
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does my employer commit an offence by acting against me` IF
    `my employer took an action listed in s 57(1)` AND (NOT `the employer proves child support played no part in it`)

`the most my employer can be fined under s 57, in penalty units` MEANS 20

§ `Part V -- when I must pay, penalties and recovery`

§§ `s 66 -- when my debt is due`

-- s 66(1): a child support debt "in respect of a day in an initial period is due and payable on the seventh day of the calendar
-- month following that day". s 66(2): one "in respect of a payment period is due and payable on the seventh day after the end of
-- the period". (s 66(3) and s 67A, lump sums and initial periods, are not encoded.)
@export When is my child support debt due?
GIVEN `it is for a day in an initial period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the debt is for a day in the initial period entered in the Register (otherwise it is for a payment period)
      `the day, or the last day of the payment period` IS A DATE @desc The day the debt is for, or the last day of the payment period
GIVETH A DATE
`When is my child support debt due` MEANS
    IF `it is for a day in an initial period`
    THEN `By when must my employer remit my deduction` `the day, or the last day of the payment period`
    ELSE `the day, or the last day of the payment period` PLUS 7

§§ `ss 67, 68 -- late payment penalty`

-- s 4(1): "payment period ... means one of the following periods: (a) a week; (b) a fortnight; (c) a 4 week period; (d) a month;
-- (e) a calendar month."
DECLARE `Payment period` IS ONE OF
    `a week`
    `a fortnight`
    `a 4 week period`
    `a month`
    `a calendar month`

-- s 67(1): if a child support debt "remains unpaid after the time when it became due and payable", the person is liable to a
-- penalty "(a) if the payment period is a week -- ... on the balance outstanding each week at a weekly rate equal to 1/52 of the
-- relevant annual rate; (b) ... a fortnight -- ... 1/26 ...; (c) ... a 4 week period -- ... 4/52 ...; (d) ... a month or a calendar
-- month -- ... 1/12 of the relevant annual rate". s 67(1A): "the balance outstanding is the total amount of one or more child
-- support debts remaining unpaid". s 67(3): "relevant annual rate means the rate that is from time to time the general interest
-- charge rate under subsection 8AAD(1) of the Taxation Administration Act 1953" -- an INPUT, as a fraction (0.11 for 11%).
GIVEN `the period` IS A `Payment period`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the share of the annual rate for one period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the period`
        WHEN `a week` THEN 1 DIVIDED BY 52
        WHEN `a fortnight` THEN 1 DIVIDED BY 26
        WHEN `a 4 week period` THEN 4 DIVIDED BY 52
        OTHERWISE 1 DIVIDED BY 12

@export What late payment penalty can I be charged for one payment period?
GIVEN `my payment period` IS A `Payment period` @desc The payment period entered in the Register for your liability
      `the balance outstanding` IS A NUMBER @desc The total of your child support debts unpaid after their due dates
      `the general interest charge rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The annual general interest charge rate for the period, as a fraction (0.11 for 11%)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What late payment penalty for one period` MEANS
    `the balance outstanding` TIMES `the general interest charge rate` TIMES `the share of the annual rate for one period` `my payment period`

-- s 68(1): the Registrar "may remit" the penalty if satisfied "(a) ... the circumstances that contributed to the delay ... were not
-- due to ... an act or omission of the person; and (ii) the person has taken reasonable action to mitigate"; or "(b) ... were due
-- to ... an act or omission of the person; and ... has taken reasonable action to mitigate ...; and (iii) ... it would be fair and
-- reasonable to remit"; or "(c) ... there are special circumstances by reason of which it would be fair and reasonable". s 68(2),
-- (3): a refusal or part remission must be notified in writing with my right to object and then to the ART.
@export Can the Registrar let me off the late payment penalty?
GIVEN `the delay was not my doing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied the circumstances causing the delay were not caused by your act or omission
      `I took reasonable action to mitigate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied you took reasonable action to mitigate those circumstances or their effects
      `it would be fair and reasonable to remit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied, given those circumstances, it would be fair and reasonable to remit
      `there are special circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied special circumstances make remission fair and reasonable
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the Registrar remit my late payment penalty` IF
        (`the delay was not my doing` AND `I took reasonable action to mitigate`)
    OR  ((NOT `the delay was not my doing`) AND `I took reasonable action to mitigate` AND `it would be fair and reasonable to remit`)
    OR  `there are special circumstances`

§§ `ss 71, 71C -- payments that count although not made to the Registrar`

-- s 71(1): if the payee "receives from the payer an amount intended by both the payer and the payee to be paid in complete or
-- partial satisfaction of an amount payable under the liability ...; and (b) the payer or the payee applies to the Registrar to
-- have the amount ... treated as having been paid to the Registrar; the Registrar must ... credit the amount". "Subject to section
-- 71D" (refusal in special circumstances: an input).
@export Will money I paid straight to the other parent be credited against my child support?
GIVEN `the amount paid to the payee` IS A NUMBER @desc What you paid the payee directly
      `we both intended it as child support` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and the payee both intended it to satisfy amounts payable under the liability
      `one of us applied to the Registrar to credit it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you or the payee applied to have it treated as paid to the Registrar
      `the Registrar refused under s 71D` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar refused to credit it in special circumstances under s 71D
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much of a direct payment must be credited` MEANS
    IF `we both intended it as child support` AND `one of us applied to the Registrar to credit it` AND (NOT `the Registrar refused under s 71D`)
    THEN `the amount paid to the payee`
    ELSE 0

-- s 71C(1): if the payer "has made one or more payments to the payee ... or to another person"; "the payment is a payment of the
-- kind specified in the regulations" (not deposited: an input); and the payer does not "have at least regular care of any of the
-- children", the Registrar "must ... credit the excess amount ... up to a maximum of 30% of the amount payable". s 71C(3): no
-- credit for a period for which the payer "has not paid to the Commonwealth an amount equal to the difference" between the amount
-- payable and the credit (s 71C(4) lets a late payment of the difference revive it). The "excess over earlier credits" in s
-- 71C(1)(c) is taken as the payments given here.
@export How much of my payments for school fees and the like (prescribed payments) must be credited this period?
GIVEN `the amount payable for the period` IS A NUMBER @desc The child support payable by you for the payment period
      `my prescribed payments for the period` IS A NUMBER @desc Payments you made of a kind the Regulations specify, not already credited
      `I have at least regular care of one of the children` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have at least regular care of any child the assessment covers
      `I paid the Commonwealth the rest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid the Commonwealth the amount payable less the credit
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much of my prescribed payments must be credited` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I have at least regular care of one of the children`) AND `I paid the Commonwealth the rest`
    THEN `the lesser of` `my prescribed payments for the period` (0.3 TIMES `the amount payable for the period`)
    ELSE 0

§§ `ss 72, 72A, 72AA -- collection from my refund, from people holding my money, from my Centrelink payment`

-- s 72(1): if the Commissioner "would be required ... to refund an amount" to a person who "owes a relevant debt", the Registrar
-- may require the Commissioner to pay the Registrar "an amount not exceeding the lesser of the refund amount and the debt".
@export How much of my tax refund can be taken for my child support debt?
GIVEN `my tax refund` IS A NUMBER @desc The refund the Commissioner of Taxation would pay you
      `my child support debt` IS A NUMBER @desc Your unpaid child support debts and related penalties
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much of my tax refund can be taken` MEANS `the lesser of` `my tax refund` `my child support debt`

-- s 72A(1): the Registrar may give written notice to a person who owes, holds or may hold money for a relevant debtor, requiring
-- payment of the money, or "an amount equal to the maximum notified deduction total" if the money is more. s 72A(1B): that total
-- "does not exceed the relevant debt". s 72A(5): I must be given a copy.
@export How much can my bank (or anyone who owes or holds money for me) be made to pay towards my child support debt?
GIVEN `the money held for me` IS A NUMBER @desc The money the person owes you or holds for you
      `the total in the notice` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum notified deduction total in the Registrar's notice
      `my child support debt` IS A NUMBER @desc Your unpaid child support debts and related penalties
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much can a third person be made to pay` MEANS
    `the lesser of` `the money held for me` (`the lesser of` `the total in the notice` `my child support debt`)

-- s 72AA(1): if the payer of a liability "covered by subsection 17(2)" (an assessment) "is in receipt of a social security pension
-- or a social security benefit", the Registrar may instruct the Secretary "to make the prescribed periodic deduction". s 72AA(2):
-- if a person "by whom a child support debt ... is due ... has not paid the debt" and receives such a payment, the Registrar may
-- instruct "(i) the prescribed periodic deduction ... until the debt is paid; or (ii) if the Registrar is satisfied the person is
-- in financial hardship -- ... the specified lesser periodic deduction". The prescribed amount is in the Regulations (not deposited).
DECLARE `Centrelink deduction` IS ONE OF
    `the prescribed periodic deduction may be taken`
    `only a lesser deduction may be taken -- financial hardship`
    `no deduction under s 72AA`

@export Can child support be deducted from my Centrelink pension or benefit?
GIVEN `I receive a social security pension or benefit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are paid a social security pension or benefit
      `the basis` IS A `Liability basis` @desc What the liability arises under
      `I have an unpaid child support debt` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a child support debt due from you is unpaid
      `the Registrar is satisfied I am in financial hardship` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied you are in financial hardship
GIVETH A `Centrelink deduction`
DECIDE `What can be deducted from my Centrelink payment` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `I receive a social security pension or benefit`
            THEN `no deduction under s 72AA`
        IF `I have an unpaid child support debt` AND `the Registrar is satisfied I am in financial hardship`
            THEN `only a lesser deduction may be taken -- financial hardship`
        IF `I have an unpaid child support debt` OR `the basis` EQUALS `a child support assessment`
            THEN `the prescribed periodic deduction may be taken`
        OTHERWISE `no deduction under s 72AA`

§§ `ss 72D-72L -- departure prohibition orders`

-- s 72E: "a person has a child support liability if: (a) the person has a registrable maintenance liability of a kind mentioned in
-- section 17 ...; (b) an amount payable under [it] is a child support debt; and (c) the day on which the debt became due and payable
-- under section 66 has passed, and the debt remains unpaid in whole or in part."
-- s 72D(1): the Registrar "may make an order ... prohibiting a person from departing from Australia for a foreign country if: (a)
-- the person has a child support liability ...; (b) the person has not made arrangements satisfactory to the Registrar for the
-- liability to be wholly discharged; (c) the Registrar is satisfied that the person has persistently and without reasonable grounds
-- failed to pay ... child support debts ...; and (d) the Registrar believes on reasonable grounds that it is desirable to make the
-- order" so I do not leave without paying or arranging to pay. s 72D(2) lists what the Registrar weighs for (c).
@export Can the Registrar stop me leaving Australia over my child support?
GIVEN `I have an overdue unpaid child support debt` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a child support debt from a registered liability is past its due date and unpaid in whole or part
      `I have made arrangements satisfactory to the Registrar` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have made arrangements the Registrar accepts for the liability to be wholly paid
      `the Registrar is satisfied I persistently failed to pay without reasonable grounds` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied you persistently, and without reasonable grounds, failed to pay
      `the Registrar believes an order is desirable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar believes on reasonable grounds an order is desirable so you do not leave without paying or arranging to pay
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can a departure prohibition order be made against me` IF
        `I have an overdue unpaid child support debt`
    AND (NOT `I have made arrangements satisfactory to the Registrar`)
    AND `the Registrar is satisfied I persistently failed to pay without reasonable grounds`
    AND `the Registrar believes an order is desirable`

-- s 72I(1): "The Registrar must revoke a departure prohibition order ... if: (a) the person no longer has a child support liability
-- ...; or (b) ... arrangements satisfactory to the Registrar have been made for the liability to be wholly discharged; or (c) ...
-- the Registrar is satisfied that the liability is completely irrecoverable." s 72I(2): not if the Registrar considers a further
-- liability may arise from past matters, unless satisfied it too will be discharged, arranged for or irrecoverable.
@export Must my departure prohibition order be revoked?
GIVEN `I no longer have a child support liability` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have no overdue unpaid child support debt left
      `I have made arrangements satisfactory to the Registrar` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether arrangements the Registrar accepts have been made to pay it all
      `the Registrar is satisfied it is completely irrecoverable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied the liability is completely irrecoverable
      `a further liability may arise and is not provided for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar considers a later liability may arise from past matters and is not satisfied it will be paid, arranged for or irrecoverable
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my departure prohibition order be revoked` IF
        (`I no longer have a child support liability` OR `I have made arrangements satisfactory to the Registrar` OR `the Registrar is satisfied it is completely irrecoverable`)
    AND (NOT `a further liability may arise and is not provided for`)

-- s 72L(2): the Registrar "must issue the departure authorisation certificate if ... satisfied: (a) that ... (i) it is likely that
-- the person will depart from Australia and return ... within a period that the Registrar considers appropriate; and (ii) it is
-- likely that ... the Registrar will be required by subsection 72I(1) to revoke the [order]; and (b) that it is not necessary for
-- the person to give security". s 72L(3): otherwise must issue if "(a) ... (i) [revocation is likely] and (ii) the person has given
-- security under section 72M"; or "(b) ... (i) ... the certificate should be issued on humanitarian grounds; or (ii) ... refusing
-- ... will be detrimental to Australia's interests".
@export Must I be given a certificate letting me travel despite the order?
GIVEN `the Registrar thinks I will leave and return in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied you are likely to leave and come back within an appropriate period
      `the Registrar thinks the order will soon have to be revoked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied it will likely have to revoke the order under s 72I(1) within an appropriate period
      `the Registrar thinks security is unnecessary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied you need not give security for your return
      `I have given security for my return` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given the security the Registrar considers appropriate (bond, deposit or otherwise)
      `humanitarian grounds or Australia's interests` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar is satisfied the certificate should issue on humanitarian grounds or refusing would be detrimental to Australia's interests
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I be given a departure authorisation certificate` IF
        (`the Registrar thinks I will leave and return in time` AND `the Registrar thinks the order will soon have to be revoked` AND `the Registrar thinks security is unnecessary`)
    OR  (`the Registrar thinks the order will soon have to be revoked` AND `I have given security for my return`)
    OR  `humanitarian grounds or Australia's interests`

-- s 72F: "A person must not depart from Australia for a foreign country if: (a) a departure prohibition order ... is in force, and
-- the person knows that the order is in force, or is reckless ...; and (b) the person's departure is not authorised by a departure
-- authorisation certificate, and the person knows [that] ... or is reckless .... Penalty: 60 penalty units or imprisonment for 12
-- months, or both." s 72H(2): an order "is not in force during any period when a deportation order ... is in force".
@export Do I commit an offence if I leave Australia while a departure prohibition order is in force?
GIVEN `an order is in force and I know it or am reckless about it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a departure prohibition order is in force and you know, or are reckless as to whether, it is
      `I have a departure authorisation certificate for this trip` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a certificate authorises this departure
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I commit an offence by leaving Australia` IF
    `an order is in force and I know it or am reckless about it` AND (NOT `I have a departure authorisation certificate for this trip`)

`the most I can be fined for leaving, in penalty units` MEANS 60
`the most I can be imprisoned for leaving, in months` MEANS 12

§§ `ss 30(3), 113, 113A -- can the other parent sue me`

-- s 30(3): once registered, "the payee ... is not entitled to, and may not enforce payment of, amounts payable under the liability
-- other than by instituting a proceeding under section 113A". s 113(1)(b): the debt "may be sued for and recovered by: (i) the
-- Registrar ...; or (ii) the payee ... suing in accordance with section 113A". s 113A(1): the payee may sue "if the payee notifies
-- the Registrar in writing of his or her intention ...: (a) at least 14 days before instituting the proceeding; or (b) in
-- exceptional circumstances -- within such shorter period as the court allows".
DECLARE `Payee suit` IS ONE OF
    `this Act does not limit it -- my liability is not registered`
    `yes -- by a s 113A proceeding, the Registrar having been told in time`
    `not yet -- the payee must first give the Registrar 14 days' written notice`

@export Can the other parent take me to court for unpaid child support?
GIVEN `my liability is registered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the liability is registered under this Act
      `days' notice the payee gave the Registrar` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days before suing the payee told the Registrar in writing
      `the court allowed a shorter period for exceptional circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court allowed a shorter notice period in exceptional circumstances
GIVETH A `Payee suit`
DECIDE `Can the payee sue me` IS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `my liability is registered`
            THEN `this Act does not limit it -- my liability is not registered`
        IF `days' notice the payee gave the Registrar` AT LEAST 14 OR `the court allowed a shorter period for exceptional circumstances`
            THEN `yes -- by a s 113A proceeding, the Registrar having been told in time`
        OTHERWISE `not yet -- the payee must first give the Registrar 14 days' written notice`

§ `Debt agreements and bankruptcy`

-- This Act's deposit mentions bankruptcy once: s 50(3), about the trustee of a bankrupt EMPLOYER. It has no provision stopping or
-- suspending collection from a payer who has a debt agreement under Part IX of the Bankruptcy Act 1966 or is bankrupt: s 43(2)
-- ("must, as far as practicable, collect ... by deduction") and s 46(1) (the employer "shall ... make a deduction") are unqualified.
-- The Bankruptcy Act 1966 s 185F(1)(c) (while a proposal is processed) and s 185K(3)(b) (while an agreement is in force) say "a
-- person who is entitled under a law of the Commonwealth ... to retain or deduct money from money that is or will be owing or
-- payable to the debtor must not retain or deduct money", without limiting it to provable debts (read in the Bankruptcy Act
-- baseline encoding, finding 4). Whether that bar reaches an employer deducting under s 46 is a question of the Bankruptcy Act read
-- against this Act, not answered by either text; it is an INPUT, so this encoding stays consistent with that encoding.
DECLARE `Deductions during a debt agreement` IS ONE OF
    `deductions continue -- this Act has no exception for a debt agreement`
    `this Act still requires them, but the Bankruptcy Act bar read literally forbids them`

@export While I have a debt agreement, do child support deductions from my pay continue?
GIVEN `a debt agreement proposal is being processed or an agreement is in force` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether acceptance of your proposal for processing is recorded on the National Personal Insolvency Index, or your agreement is in force and on it
      `the Bankruptcy Act bar is taken to reach child support deductions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether Bankruptcy Act s 185F(1)(c) / s 185K(3)(b) is read literally, so that it reaches deductions under this Act
GIVETH A `Deductions during a debt agreement`
DECIDE `Do my child support deductions continue during my debt agreement` IS
    IF `a debt agreement proposal is being processed or an agreement is in force` AND `the Bankruptcy Act bar is taken to reach child support deductions`
    THEN `this Act still requires them, but the Bankruptcy Act bar read literally forbids them`
    ELSE `deductions continue -- this Act has no exception for a debt agreement`
